ression \"(not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and Halted\". Inside parentheses: not A or not B or C. In Python, not > or, so (not A) or (not B) or C. That's correct. Then and Halted. So if invalid true and Halted true. Good.\n\nExpression \"(not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and not Halted\". Precedence: parentheses, then not Halted, then and. So invalid and not Halted. Good.\n\nExpression \"AtGoal and Released and not Halted\": Python parses as AtGoal and Released and (not Halted) because not > and. Good.\n\nExpression \"not OwnReservation and not Released\": parses as (not OwnReservation) and (not Released). Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"AtGoal and Released and not Halted\" could be parsed as AtGoal and Released and (not Halted). Correct.\n\nNow, is there any requirement that step expressions cannot refer to mode flags in conditions? They can. We use ModeBrakeRequested and ModeStopped. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"Current mode is exactly one of Idle, Waiting, Traversing, BrakeRequested, Stopped, ResumePending, Done. A step expression may also refer to ModeIdle, ModeWaiting, ModeTraversing, ModeBrakeRequested, ModeStopped, ModeResumePending, ModeDone. Exactly one such mode flag is true.\" We use.\n\nPotential issue: \"No special terminal-mode shortcut exists: the requirements apply in every mode, including Done.\" Our default True -> Proceed Traversing applies to Done if no higher. So ModeDone with no higher will Proceed Traversing. Is that intended? The policy says ordinary step outcome is Proceed entering Traversing. It applies to every mode. So yes, even Done. But wait, if current mode Done, should it maybe not proceed? The policy says no terminal-mode shortcut; requirements apply in every mode. The ordinary outcome is Proceed entering Traversing. So if in Done and no exceptions, it proceeds to Traversing. That seems odd but per instruction. So yes.\n\nPotential issue: \"The ordinary step outcome is Proceed, en